// CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Spider Text-to-SQL needs exactly one SQL query inside a single <<...>> span,
// with no free text outside it. This strategy therefore tracks whether we are
// already inside the constrained SQL span and, if so, the current constrained
// prefix currentConstrainedOut that mirrors the suffix of generated after the
// opening delimiter.
// 
// Before the span opens, we strongly prefer opening it immediately: if nothing
// has been generated yet, we force "<<"; otherwise we sample unconstrained but
// heavily boost "<<" so the model quickly enters the SQL block. Inside the
// span, we use parser-aware decoding: if the current SQL prefix is complete we
// close with ">>"; otherwise we look for parser-valid next tokens, boost a few
// top valid candidates, and sample. If the sampled token is invalid we fall
// back to ConstrainedStep, which returns a parser-valid continuation (or EOS).
// This keeps generation schema-aware while still letting the LM express
// preferences among valid SQL continuations.
// 
// The tracked constrained prefix is what makes the validity argument simple:
// parser validity is preserved because only parser-valid tokens are appended to
// currentConstrainedOut, and generated always ends with the same constrained
// content while inside the span. That suffix relationship is exactly what ties
// the full emitted answer <<SQL>> to the parser-checked SQL interior.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the forced-open and boosted-open unconstrained branches,
// entering the span sets currentConstrainedOut := [], and parser.IsValidPrefix([])
// holds by precondition. In the ordinary unconstrained non-open branch we remain
// outside, so the implication is vacuous. In the complete-prefix branch,
// CloseConstrainedSpan sets insideConstrainedOut to false, again making the
// implication vacuous. In the soft constrained branch, if the sampled token is
// valid we call AppendConstrainedToken, which preserves validity; if it is not
// valid we do not append it and instead use ConstrainedStep, whose result is
// appended only when not EOS, preserving validity.
// 
// suffix: The open-span branches append "<<" to generated and set
// currentConstrainedOut := [], so the length-0 suffix matches []. The ordinary
// unconstrained non-open branch stays outside, so no suffix obligation applies.
// CloseConstrainedSpan appends only the closing delimiter and resets the
// constrained prefix to [], so the implication is vacuous afterwards. In the
// constrained append branches, AppendConstrainedToken appends the same token to
// generated and currentConstrainedOut, preserving the suffix equality.
// 
// cost: Forced-open uses no cost-bumping helper and increments only steps, so
// helpers.cost <= steps remains true. Boosted unconstrained sampling uses the
